## 2. GGUF Split: Reject Non-Contiguous Shard Numbers

**Component:** `include/strata/artifact/gguf_split.hpp` — `gguf_split_paths()`

**Responsibilities:** Resolves any shard path of a split model into the ordered list of all shard paths, verifying each exists on disk.

**Problem:** The function rewrites only the five-digit shard number and checks existence, but does not verify that the parsed `no` (the shard number in the input path) is consistent with the file actually being one of the resolved shards. A path like `model-00003-of-00004.gguf` where shard 3 is a symlink to shard 1's content would pass the existence check but produce a model with duplicate tensors caught only later by `GgufModel`'s duplicate-name check, with a confusing error. More critically, if a user passes `model-00000-of-00004.gguf` (zero-padded but out of range), the `no < 1` guard returns `{any}` silently, treating it as a single-shard model.

**Proposed change:** After resolving all shard paths, verify the input path appears in the resolved list.

```diff
--- a/include/strata/artifact/gguf_split.hpp
+++ b/include/strata/artifact/gguf_split.hpp
@@ -38,6 +38,10 @@ inline std::vector<std::string> gguf_split_paths(const std::string& any) {
         if (!std::ifstream(p, std::ios::binary))
             throw std::runtime_error("missing model shard " + p + " (shard " + std::to_string(i) + " of " +
                                      std::to_string(n) + "; is the download complete?)");
         out.push_back(std::move(p));
     }
+    // The input path must be one of the resolved shards; a path that parses as a split
+    // name but is not in the family (e.g. a renamed file) would silently produce a model
+    // missing the shard the caller actually opened.
+    if (std::find(out.begin(), out.end(), any) == out.end())
+        throw std::runtime_error("GGUF: '" + any + "' parses as shard " + std::to_string(no) + " of " +
+                                 std::to_string(n) + " but is not among the resolved shards");
     return out;
 }
```

**Verification cases:**
1. **Renamed shard:** Create a directory with `model-00001-of-00002.gguf` and `model-00002-of-00002.gguf`, then copy shard 1 to `model-00003-of-00002.gguf`. `gguf_split_paths("model-00003-of-00002.gguf")` must throw because shard 3 > n=2 causes the `no > n` guard to return `{any}`, but a path like `model-00001-of-00002.gguf` renamed to `model-00001-of-00003.gguf` (where shard 3 exists as a copy) must throw the new error.
2. **Normal 4-shard model:** The Unsloth UD-Q4_K_XL test fixture (4 shards). `gguf_split_paths` on any of the four paths must return all four in order without throwing.

